WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.840
The air around you is mostly nitrogen.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.880
So why does this cornfield need nitrogen added, while the soybeans next door often

3
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:12.880
do not?

4
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.190
First, why does a plant need nitrogen at all?

5
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.870
A protein is a large molecule that builds cells and does much of its work.

6
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.630
Proteins contain nitrogen.

7
00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.310
DNA, which carries the plant's instructions, contains nitrogen too.

8
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:36.070
So without nitrogen, a plant cannot build new proteins or grow new leaves.

9
00:00:37.183 --> 00:00:39.743
Now pull back to the sky above one field.

10
00:00:40.863 --> 00:00:43.303
It holds a huge amount of nitrogen gas.

11
00:00:44.523 --> 00:00:47.263
The soybeans growing there hold only a tiny bit.

12
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:50.437
But corn cannot use nitrogen gas.

13
00:00:51.797 --> 00:00:53.117
Neither can a deer, or you.

14
00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:57.937
Grey pairs drift down into the soil, right past the corn's roots.

15
00:00:59.257 --> 00:01:00.757
Each pair stays clasped shut.

16
00:01:01.957 --> 00:01:02.977
The gate is locked.

17
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.590
Plants can use only usable nitrogen.

18
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:12.750
That is nitrogen changed out of the gas into small particles that plants can take

19
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.950
in. Roots take most from soil water.

20
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.490
Next to the sky, this green pool is small.

21
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:22.033
So who opens the gate?

22
00:01:23.653 --> 00:01:25.393
Lightning opens it a little, in a flash.

23
00:01:26.773 --> 00:01:28.913
Factories that make fertilizer open it wide.

24
00:01:30.293 --> 00:01:34.273
Fertilizer is material farmers add to soil to supply nutrients.

25
00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:37.567
Some bacteria open the gate too.

26
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:42.147
Bacteria are tiny living things, each made of one cell.

27
00:01:43.407 --> 00:01:44.967
Some kinds live free in the soil.

28
00:01:46.307 --> 00:01:48.747
Each changer turns grey pairs into green dots.

29
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.120
Now picture soil with almost no usable nitrogen.

30
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.640
Corn and soybeans stand in the same air.

31
00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:59.420
Which one could still grow well?

32
00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:01.740
Pause and decide.

33
00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:12.103
The soybean. Dig it up and shake off the soil.

34
00:02:13.423 --> 00:02:15.363
See the small round bumps on its roots?

35
00:02:16.723 --> 00:02:19.803
Each one is a root nodule, and it is packed with bacteria.

36
00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:27.037
Inside the nodule, the bacteria take in nitrogen gas and pass usable nitrogen to the

37
00:02:27.037 --> 00:02:31.877
plant. The soybean carries its own gate opener, right on its roots.

38
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.290
The plant sends sugar from its leaves down to the nodules.

39
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.210
Root cells there change the sugar into food the bacteria can use.

40
00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:43.983
Corn has no root nodules.

41
00:02:45.263 --> 00:02:48.063
It can draw only on the green dots already in the soil.

42
00:02:49.243 --> 00:02:52.903
That is why corn farmers usually add nitrogen to their fields.

43
00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.667
So the air's nitrogen is not the problem.

44
00:02:58.007 --> 00:03:03.887
The gate is. Plants need usable nitrogen, not the nitrogen gas in the air.

45
00:03:05.107 --> 00:03:09.667
Where nitrogen runs short, usable nitrogen sets how much a field can grow.

46
00:03:10.717 --> 00:03:13.857
But do the bumps really add nitrogen to a soybean crop?

47
00:03:15.137 --> 00:03:17.377
The lesson tests that with real field data.
